<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vande Mataram</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">written in the 1870s, probably around <a href="/years/1876">1876</a>; first published <a href="/years/1882">1882</a> in the novel Anandamath</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as বন্দে মাতরম্, Bande Mataram</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Vande Mataram&quot; is a poem written in Bengali, with many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> words, by Bankim Chandra Chatterjee, one of the first graduates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> University, who served the colonial government as a Deputy Collector and later a Deputy Magistrate.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Bankim Chandra Chatterjee was one of the earliest graduates of the newly founded Calcutta University and served the colonial government as a Deputy Collector and then a Deputy Magistrate. He was much interested in the Revolt of 1857 and the Sanyasi Rebellion, and at about the same time the administration was trying to promote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a> as an anthem for Indian subjects, which nationalists disliked. The idea of the poem is generally believed to have come to him around 1876, while he was still an official.</p><p>He wrote it at Chinsura, on the Hooghly river, in a spontaneous session, using words from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Bengali. It was published in 1882 in his novel Anandamath, which is set during the Sannyasi Rebellion and has been noted for its pro-British sentiments. Jadunath Bhattacharya was asked to set a tune to it soon after it was written.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The poem became one of the most popular songs of protest in the independence movement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a> sang it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> in 1896, and the Congress adopted it in 1905 as a salutation to Mother India. The colonial government banned the book and made public recital a crime, but crowds repeatedly sang it in front of officials. On 20 May 1906 more than ten thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Muslims marched at Barisal behind Vande Mataram flags, and in 1907 Bhikaiji Cama's early flag of India, made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, carried the words.</p><p>In 1937 a Congress committee of Maulana Azad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a>, Acharya Deva and Tagore recommended using only the first two stanzas, because later stanzas refer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>; Tagore wrote that the song could not unite all communities, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> opposed it. On 24 January 1950 the Constituent Assembly, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-prasad">Rajendra Prasad</a> presiding, resolved that it should be honoured equally with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jana-gana-mana">Jana Gana Mana</a>. Sri Aurobindo's prose translation appeared in Karmayogin on 20 November 1909, and the oldest surviving recordings date from 1907.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vande Mataram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 24 January 1950 the Constituent Assembly adopted the first stanza as the national anthem, with &quot;<b>Vande Mataram</b>&quot; honoured equally as the national song.</blockquote><a href="/songs/jana-gana-mana">Jana Gana Mana</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Vande Mataram</b>&quot; is a poem written in Bengali, with many Sanskrit words, by Bankim Chandra Chatterjee, one of the first graduates of Calcutta University, who served the colonial government as a Deputy Collector and later a Deputy Magistrate.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li></ul></section>
